#!/bin/sh
# A docker CLI that is not one.
#
# THIS IS WHAT MAKES THE SUITE RUN WITH NO DAEMON. `qits.system.docker.binary` is a config key
# precisely so a test can point it at this file: as far as the service is concerned, `docker` is a
# program on PATH, and here the suite gets to choose which program that is. Nothing is mocked
# inside the service — a real ProcessBuilder forks a real child, a real PTY carries a real
# terminal, and the argv this receives is the argv the argv builders produced.
#
# It answers three kinds of call:
#
#   reads       recorded output, in the exact shapes docker prints (one JSON object per line for
#               `ls --format`, a JSON array for `inspect`). They are abridged captures of the
#               fixtures in system/src/test/resources/fixtures, which is where the real ones live.
#   refusals    an unknown container exits 1 with docker's own wording, so the service's
#               404-versus-503 rule is exercised rather than assumed.
#   terminals   `run -it` and `exec -it` become a real echo loop with a WINCH trap, so a websocket
#               test drives a genuine pseudo-terminal end to end.
#
# Every call is appended to calls.log beside this script, so a test can assert WHICH command was
# run — the boot sweep's `rm -f`, the glances teardown — without a mock.

here=$(dirname "$0")
printf '%s\n' "$*" >> "$here/calls.log"

# THE SECOND RECORDING, and the one a userflow story reads. calls.log above is the argv at the
# moment the call STARTED — one line, space-joined, which is the shape the surefire suite asserts
# on and which nothing here changes. results.log carries the same argv with THE ANSWER beside it,
# `<exit><TAB><arg><TAB><arg>…`, because "the call was answered" is the other half of the evidence:
# a diagram drawn from the first file could only say a call was made, and a refusal — `container
# inspect` on a name no daemon knows — is exactly a call that was made and said no.
#
# THE TWO INTERACTIVE VERBS RECORD ONCE, AT SPAWN, with the word `running` where a code would go.
# An `exec -it` is not a completed call: it is a child on a pseudo-terminal that is still there
# when the story looks at it, and the service ends it with SIGTERM (TerminalSession.terminate),
# which no EXIT trap is guaranteed to survive. A code recorded there would be a code that sometimes
# exists, which is worse than no code at all — see StoryDocker, which turns these lines into edges.
tab=$(printf '\t')
argv_tabbed=""
for a in "$@"; do argv_tabbed="$argv_tabbed$tab$a"; done
record() { printf '%s%s\n' "$1" "$argv_tabbed" >> "$here/results.log"; }
case "${1:-}" in
  exec|run) record running ;;
  *) trap 'record "$?"' EXIT ;;
esac

# The container this fake knows about, and the id it answers with. 64 hex, because that is what the
# daemon prints and the only thing the exec argv is allowed to carry.
